  "_doc": "Parts in local metres (+z faces the road or the arriving traffic, base at y = 0). shape box (min, max), cylinder (base, radius, height, segments) or blob (center, radius); glow 'night' lights the part at night. Signal lenses: lens 0/1/2 = the cars' red/amber/green (on both faces of the head), 3/4 = the walkers' stop/walk (their head faces -x, across the crosswalk; the cars' red and green). color is the lens lit as the screen shows it (render/tone_map.gd gives the radiance): the colour regions of road traffic signals (CIE, as tabled by Janoff, Transportation Research Record 1316, 1991, Table 1; EN 12368 Table 7 asks the same) lie outside what an sRGB screen shows for red and amber, so each is the brightest colour the Environment's ACES can show whose chromaticity is within one MacAdam step (0.0011 in CIE 1976 u'v', CIE TN 001:2014) of the nearest one it can show (red: 0.0377 from the region, the nearest 0.0366; amber: 0.0073, the nearest 0.0062); green the brightest inside its region. An unlit lens is that colour times lens_dim (sRGB), a dark filter lit by the scene: in full sun (daylight.json sun and ambient) it stays at least 16 times dimmer than the lens lit, the phantom signal class 5 of EN 12368 (ratios 21.2, 16.6 and 19.9). The signal head: three 12-inch sections (MUTCD 4D.07; 300 mm lenses, EN 12368 6.2), each housing 13.5 x 13.5 x 7.0 in (SWARCO McCain traffic signal housing sheet), two back to back for the two faces, its bottom 15 ft over the road (MUTCD 4D.15), red on top; each lens a dome 2.0 in proud of its door (Leotek IL6-P3 12-inch module), under a cap visor as deep as the lens is wide, 0.10 in thick (McCain visor sheet), dull black (MUTCD 4D.12). The walkers' head: two 8-inch sections (10 x 10 x 7.0 in, 200 mm lenses, domes 1.7 in, 8-inch visors), the upraised hand above the walking person (MUTCD 4E.04), its bottom at 8.5 ft, the middle of MUTCD 4E.05's 7 to 10 ft. Housings and visors black: linear albedo 0.05, a black-pigmented polymer (Physically Based, 'Toner (Black)'). The lenses are not in the post's mesh: each is drawn once, lit or not, by render/props/signal_lamps.h.",
